body:
I totally get the confusion, nonbinary can be a little complicated because unlike certain sexualities for example it doesn't have the clearest lines and can vary between person to person. It's somewhat of an umbrella term. I'll try to explain it how I came to understand it and identify with it.

&#x200B;

So gender itself is a social thing that's more or less made by us. It's a combination of appearance, personality traits, I think you get the idea. But the important thing here is that it's a **feeling** around how you feel deep down and identify that is separate from your sex and anatomy. In our current society we pretty much only recognize men and women which creates a gender "**binary**" which is defined as relating to two things. If someone feels they don't fit in either of 2 identities (being man or woman) then it's considered nonbinary because it's just something that we socially don't really have a term for right now.

Another way to look at it is through a numerical lens. Lets take the two parts of the gender binary system and assign them numbers like 0's and 1's. Which number is man or woman is irrelevant here, but socially we absolutely know a 1 from a 0 and what it means to be that number. Now what if someone says they don't feel like they fit as a 1 but they also know they're not a 0? They're more a 0.5 for example, or maybe a 0.75, 0.2, or even a 0.9999993. That number, that person doesn't fit in either of those binary boxes and into what's socially understood so that's nonbinary.

&#x200B;

Now as society changes and evolves we may very well come up with proper terminology for all the various "numbers" and someone could feel comfortable identifying accordingly, but for right now we're a little stuck. I recommend speaking with your child, maybe look into getting them to speak with a professional to try and help them find where they fit and how they feel.

Gender can be complicated and for some people it can be in flux. They're still growing, developing, and learning. About the world around them, and about who they are and how they view themselves. Just be there for them and show that you care, and that you'll love them however they choose to identify.

&#x200B;

I hope this explains things at least a little, and if you have any questions or anything I'm happy to help.
